# Executed inside src/build_report.py (shares its helpers). Writes docs/viva_qa.md; every number is loaded via the helpers.
section("Viva Q&A")
V = ["# Viva questions and answers (30)\n", "*Numbers in the answers are loaded from the saved results (see `docs/numbers_provenance.md`). Learning project, not a medical tool. 'Women' = participants; 'n' is stated where a result is given.*\n"]
qn = [0]
def qa(theme, q, a):
    if theme: V.append(f"\n## {theme}\n")
    qn[0] += 1; V.append(f"**Q{qn[0]}. {q}**\n\n{a}\n")
k_raw, k_log, k_std = kv("pooled", "raw (unscaled, raw hormones)"), kv("pooled", "log1p hormones, unscaled"), kv("pooled", "log1p + standardised")
npw, nlw = nn("pooled"), nn("long")
n_holm_neg = agg(D6, "comparison == 'B vs baseline' and p_holm < 0.05", None, "count", "{:.0f}", label="negctrl B vs baseline Holm<0.05")
n_b_neg = agg(D6, "comparison == 'B vs baseline'", None, "count", "{:.0f}", label="B vs baseline negctrl comparisons")
qa("A. Ridge, optimisation and the maths",
   "What is ridge regression and why use it on this data?",
   f"Least squares chooses the weights that make the squared prediction errors smallest. Ridge adds a penalty lambda times the sum of squared weights, so large weights are 'taxed'. Here the predictors overlap (phase, estradiol and progesterone move together), the sample is small ({npw}) and the targets are noisy, so plain least squares can give unstable weights. Ridge gives up a little accuracy on the training data for much more stable weights on new women. The strength lambda was chosen by nested cross-validation, never on the test woman.")
qa(None, "Derive the normal equations and explain why ridge always has a solution.",
   "Write the loss as L(w) = (1/n)||y - Xw||^2 + lambda||w||^2 on centred data. Differentiate: grad L = -(2/n)X'(y - Xw) + 2 lambda w. Set it to zero: (X'X + n lambda I) w = X'y. For lambda = 0 these are the ordinary normal equations, which fail when X'X is singular (collinear columns). Adding n lambda to the diagonal makes every eigenvalue at least n lambda > 0, so the matrix is invertible and the solution is unique. (scikit-learn's `alpha` equals n times my lambda; that is the only difference in convention.)")
qa(None, "How does the SVD give the ridge solution, and what is the pseudo-inverse?",
   f"Write X = U S V' with singular values s_i. Then X'X + n lambda I = V(S^2 + n lambda I)V', so w = V diag(s_i/(s_i^2 + n lambda)) U'y. Each direction of the data is shrunk by the factor s_i^2/(s_i^2 + n lambda): well-determined directions (large s_i) are barely touched, weak directions are suppressed. For lambda = 0 the factor becomes 1/s_i, i.e. w = V S^+ U'y, the Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse solution (tiny singular values are dropped, which gives the minimum-norm least-squares answer when X is rank-deficient). I checked both routes against scikit-learn on {agg(P3A, '', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='number of parity configurations')} configurations: the largest coefficient difference was {agg(P3A, '', 'd_svd', 'max', '{:.1e}', label='max |w_svd - sklearn|')}.")
qa(None, "Why is the intercept not penalised and why do you centre the data?",
   "The intercept only says where the average outcome sits; shrinking it towards zero would make the model depend on the arbitrary unit of the outcome (a DRSP score far from zero would be pulled towards 0). Centring X and y makes the intercept equal to the mean of y and removes it from the optimisation, so the penalty acts on the real weights only. At prediction time the training mean is added back.")
qa(None, "What are the gradient and the Hessian of the ridge loss? Is the problem convex?",
   f"Gradient: -(2/n)X'(y - Xw) + 2 lambda w. Hessian: H = (2/n)X'X + 2 lambda I. H does not depend on w: the loss is a quadratic bowl. H is positive semi-definite for lambda = 0 and positive definite for lambda > 0, so the bowl is (strictly) convex with one minimum. The eigenvalues of H are 2(s_i^2/n + lambda): ridge lifts all of them by 2 lambda. Check: gradient descent started at zero and driven by exactly this gradient reached the closed-form solution (relative suboptimality {val(P3B, dict(sample='pooled', optimizer='GD', lr_mult=1.0), 'final_rel_subopt', '{:.1e}', label='GD at 1/lmax')} after the iteration budget).")
qa(None, "What is the condition number and what did you find?",
   f"kappa = largest eigenvalue / smallest eigenvalue of H. It measures how stretched the bowl is: a long thin valley (large kappa) makes gradient descent zig-zag. With unscaled features (raw hormones in pg/ml next to 0/1 phase dummies) kappa was {k_raw} (pooled, {npw}); log1p hormones without scaling {k_log}; log1p plus standardisation {k_std}. Plain GD (step 1/lambda_max) needed {gi('pooled', 'log1p + standardised')} iterations to shrink the loss gap to {TOL} on standardised data, {gi('pooled', 'log1p hormones, unscaled')} on log1p-unscaled, and did not converge within the iteration cap on the raw data ({gi('pooled', 'raw (unscaled, raw hormones)')}). Rule of thumb: iterations are about kappa times a constant. Standardising is therefore essential for gradient methods, not only for a fair penalty.")
qa(None, "Compare GD, SGD, momentum and Adam. What did the learning-rate study show?",
   f"GD uses the exact gradient each step. SGD uses a random mini-batch ({const('src/phase3b_optim.py', r'batch=(\d+)', 'SGD batch size')} rows): cheaper, noisy; with a constant step it never settles, it stalls at a noise floor that grows with the step (relative suboptimality {val(P3B, dict(sample='pooled', optimizer='SGD', lr_mult=0.01), 'final_rel_subopt', '{:.1e}', label='SGD lr 0.01/lmax')} at a small step versus {val(P3B, dict(sample='pooled', optimizer='SGD', lr_mult=1.0), 'final_rel_subopt', '{:.1e}', label='SGD lr 1/lmax')} at a large one). Momentum keeps a running velocity to smooth the zig-zag. Adam rescales each weight's step by running averages of the gradient and its square. For GD the theory says stability needs lr < 2/lambda_max: at 1.9/lambda_max it converged (relative suboptimality {val(P3B, dict(sample='pooled', optimizer='GD', lr_mult=1.9), 'final_rel_subopt', '{:.1e}', label='GD at 1.9/lmax')}), at 2.1/lambda_max the result was: {val(P3B, dict(sample='pooled', optimizer='GD', lr_mult=2.1), 'final_rel_subopt', '{:.1e}', label='GD at 2.1/lmax')}. All four methods were run on the same loss and compared with the closed form ({npw}).")
qa(None, "Why does tuned momentum help?",
   f"On a quadratic bowl the heavy-ball method with lr = 4/(sqrt(L)+sqrt(mu))^2 and beta = ((sqrt(kappa)-1)/(sqrt(kappa)+1))^2 needs about sqrt(kappa) steps instead of kappa. Intuition: GD bounces between the steep walls of a narrow valley; the velocity term lets the ball roll along the valley floor. Measured (pooled, {npw}): on log1p-unscaled data {val(P3BM, dict(sample='pooled', problem='log1p hormones, unscaled'), 'hb_iters', '{:,.0f}', label='tuned momentum iterations, log1p, pooled')} iterations versus {val(P3BM, dict(sample='pooled', problem='log1p hormones, unscaled'), 'gd_iters', '{:,.0f}', label='GD iterations, log1p, pooled')} for GD; on raw data {val(P3BM, dict(sample='pooled', problem='raw (raw hormones, unscaled)'), 'hb_iters', '{:,.0f}', label='tuned momentum iterations, raw, pooled')} iterations where GD did not converge. The observed counts were {calc('ratio observed/theory tuned momentum, raw pooled', float(val(P3BM, dict(sample='pooled', problem='raw (raw hormones, unscaled)'), 'hb_iters', '{:.0f}')) / float(val(P3BM, dict(sample='pooled', problem='raw (raw hormones, unscaled)'), 'hb_theory', '{:.0f}')), '{:.1f}', P3BM, 'hb_iters / hb_theory, raw pooled')} times the rough theory on the raw problem, because the theory describes the asymptotic rate.")
qa(None, "Show that ridge is the MAP estimate under a Gaussian prior.",
   f"Assume y = Xw + noise, noise ~ N(0, sigma^2), and a prior w ~ N(0, tau^2 I). By Bayes, posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior, so -log posterior = ||y - Xw||^2/(2 sigma^2) + ||w||^2/(2 tau^2) + constant. Multiply by 2 sigma^2: minimise ||y - Xw||^2 + (sigma^2/tau^2)||w||^2. That is ridge with n lambda = sigma^2/tau^2: a stronger belief that weights are small (small tau^2) or noisier data (large sigma^2) means more shrinkage; a flat prior gives ordinary least squares. Numerical check: at the ridge solution the gradient of the negative log posterior had maximum absolute value {val(P3E, dict(sample='pooled'), 'max_abs_grad_neg_log_posterior', '{:.1e}', label='MAP check gradient')} (n = {val(P3E, dict(sample='pooled'), 'n_rows', '{:.0f}', label='MAP check n')} rows), i.e. zero.")
qa(None, "What does the bias-variance analysis show and how was lambda chosen?",
   f"With more shrinkage (larger lambda) the predictions vary less from sample to sample (variance falls) but are systematically off (bias rises). I bootstrapped women and averaged over held-out splits; the expected squared error was smallest at lambda = {calc('best lambda pooled', b1.lam, '{:.2g}', P3D, 'row with minimum expected_sq_err, sample == pooled')} (pooled, {npw}) and {calc('best lambda long', b2.lam, '{:.2g}', P3D, 'row with minimum expected_sq_err, sample == long')} (longitudinal, {nlw}), but the curve is shallow. On real data the true function is unknown, so bias squared and irreducible noise cannot be separated: the plot shows their sum. For the final models lambda was selected inside each training set by nested cross-validation, never by the test woman.")
qa("B. Validation, leakage and uncertainty",
   "What is leave-one-participant-out CV and what is leakage?",
   f"Each woman has up to three sessions that are similar to each other. If sessions of one woman were split between training and test, the model could recognise the woman instead of learning a general rule and would look better than it is: that is leakage. In leave-one-participant-out CV all sessions of one woman are held out together ({n_wom('pooled')} folds pooled, {n_wom('long')} folds longitudinal) and the model is trained on the other women only. Other guards: scaling and centring fitted on training rows only; hyper-parameters chosen inside the training women; no outcome or other mood score among the features; no duplicate (woman, session) rows; cross-sectional and longitudinal women are disjoint. These are asserted in the code (`docs/leakage_checklist.md`). As an empirical test, shuffling the target gave a skill between {calc('shuffled-target skill min', sh_all.min(), '{:+.4f}', SH, 'min of R2_oos over all permutations')} and {calc('shuffled-target skill max', sh_all.max(), '{:+.4f}', SH, 'max of R2_oos over all permutations')} (pooled, {npw}), i.e. none, as it should be.")
qa(None, "Why nested cross-validation?",
   f"Choosing lambda (or tree settings) by looking at the same data that scores the model gives optimistic results. Nesting separates the jobs: the inner loop ({const('src/cvlib.py', r'k_inner=(\d+)', 'inner CV folds')}-fold, grouped by woman) picks hyper-parameters using only training women; the outer loop (leave-one-woman-out) scores the model on a woman who played no role in any choice.")
qa(None, "Why a global-mean baseline, what is R2_oos, and why can R2 be negative?",
   f"The simplest honest model predicts the training mean for everyone. R2_oos = 1 - SSE_model/SSE_baseline says how much better than that baseline the model is on unseen women: 0 = no better, positive = better, negative = worse. Negative values happen when a model fits noise in the training data and then predicts worse than the mean. Example: pooled anxiety, random forest on hormones only: {m4ci('pooled', 'stai', 'rf', 'B')} ({nm4('pooled', 'stai')}).")
qa(None, "Why does the bootstrap resample women rather than rows?",
   "Rows of one woman are not independent. Resampling rows would make the sample look larger than it is and give too-narrow intervals. Resampling whole women (with all their sessions) keeps the real dependence. I used percentile intervals from the resampled metrics, and the same resampled women for paired comparisons between models, so the difference between two models has its own interval.")
qa(None, "What is the ICC and why does it matter for prediction?",
   f"The intraclass correlation is the share of a target's variance that is between women (a stable personal level) rather than within a woman across sessions. In the longitudinal sample ({nlw}) it was {ci(ICC, dict(sample='long', target='drsp_psych'), 'ICC_anova', '{:.2f}', lo='ci_lo', hi='ci_hi', label='ICC long drsp')} for DRSP psychological symptoms, {ci(ICC, dict(sample='long', target='pos_affect'), 'ICC_anova', '{:.2f}', lo='ci_lo', hi='ci_hi', label='ICC long pa')} for positive affect and {ci(ICC, dict(sample='long', target='stai'), 'ICC_anova', '{:.2f}', lo='ci_lo', hi='ci_hi', label='ICC long stai')} for anxiety. A large ICC says that most of what makes one woman different from another is stable and cannot be predicted from where she is in her cycle. For an unseen woman only person-level predictors (trait questionnaires) can reach that part.")
qa(None, "Why log1p for hormones and what about the outliers and zeros?",
   f"Estradiol and progesterone are right-skewed (skewness {dcalc('E2 skew raw', sk('estradiol', 'raw'), '{:.2f}', 'scipy.stats.skew on estradiol')} and {dcalc('P skew raw', sk('progesterone', 'raw'), '{:.2f}', 'skew of progesterone')} raw; {dcalc('E2 skew log1p', sk('estradiol', 'log'), '{:.2f}', 'skew of log1p(estradiol)')} and {dcalc('P skew log1p', sk('progesterone', 'log'), '{:.2f}', 'skew of log1p(progesterone)')} after log1p), and a few progesterone values are very large, which would dominate a linear model and inflate the condition number. {dcalc('zeros in E2', int((A.estradiol==0).sum()), how='count estradiol==0')} estradiol and {dcalc('zeros in P', int((A.progesterone==0).sum()), how='count progesterone==0')} progesterone readings are exactly zero, so log(x) is undefined; log1p = log(1 + x) handles zeros. Trees do not care about monotone transforms. I did not delete or clip the outliers.")
qa(None, "What do the prediction results say about the research question?",
   f"Hormones do not help beyond phase. Comparing phase + hormones (set C) with phase alone (set A) in {n_ca_all} paired comparisons (3 samples x 3 targets x 3 models), {n_ca_better} showed a significant improvement and {n_ca_worse} were significantly worse. Phase alone helped a little only for DRSP (pooled, ridge, {nm4('pooled', 'drsp_psych')}: {cA['drsp_psych']}). The answer is 'no detectable added value of hormones in unseen women on this sample', with the caveat that small effects cannot be excluded with {n_wom('pooled')} women.")
qa(None, "What are feature sets D and D2 and what did the comparison show?",
   f"D = phase + hormones + PSST + BDI + BAI; D2 = D without PSST. PSST and DRSP are both premenstrual-symptom questionnaires, so PSST might simply repeat the outcome. Pooled DRSP, ridge: D gives {cD['drsp_psych']}, D2 gives {cD2} ({npw}). D was significantly better than D2 in {agg(D4, 'comparison == \"D vs D2\" and metric == \"RMSE\" and delta < 0 and hi < 0', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='D vs D2 significant improvements')} of {agg(D4, 'comparison == \"D vs D2\" and metric == \"RMSE\"', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='D vs D2 comparisons')} comparisons. So PSST is not what drives the skill; BDI and BAI are. Their link to DRSP and STAI may still be partly 'one self-report explaining another', so I do not present it as early warning.")
qa(None, "What did the leave-one-session-out experiment show?",
   f"There I hold out one session of a woman and let the model see her other sessions. Giving the model her own mean from the other sessions as a feature lifted DRSP skill from {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C (no history)')} to {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C + personal mean')} and anxiety from {lo_('stai', 'ridge C (no history)')} to {lo_('stai', 'ridge C + personal mean')} (longitudinal, {val(LO, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='global mean'), 'n_rows', '{:.0f}', label='LOSO n rows')} rows, {val(LO, dict(target='drsp_psych', model='global mean'), 'n_women', '{:.0f}', label='LOSO n women')} women). The personal mean was built without the held-out session (and without the row itself for training rows) to avoid leakage. Message: knowing the person helps much more than knowing her hormones, but it needs earlier measurements of the same woman.")
qa("C. Statistics",
   "Why a mixed-effects model with a random intercept?",
   f"Sessions of one woman are correlated. A random intercept gives each woman her own baseline, so the phase effect is estimated from comparisons within each woman and the standard errors respect the dependence. Ordinary regression would treat {n_rows('long')} sessions of {n_wom('long')} women as {n_rows('long')} independent people and overstate certainty. I fitted the models with REML in statsmodels on z-scored outcomes (as the authors' R script does), so effects are in standard-deviation units.")
qa(None, "How can a significant in-sample phase effect coexist with out-of-sample R2 near zero for unseen women?",
   f"A p-value asks whether an average effect differs from zero; R2 asks how much of the variation between individual observations the effect explains. Phase explains only a small fraction (marginal R2 {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 DRSP long')} for DRSP psychological, {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='stai'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 stai long')} for anxiety, longitudinal, {nlw}), and the mixed model's test is made within women, after removing each woman's baseline, which a new woman does not bring. Estimation noise further lowers out-of-sample R2. Phase alone gives out-of-sample R2_oos of {m4ci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'ridge', 'A')} for DRSP and {m4ci('long', 'stai', 'ridge', 'A')} for anxiety although the in-sample Wald tests give p {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'wald_p_phase', 'p', label='Wald p DRSP long')} and {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='stai'), 'wald_p_phase', 'p', label='Wald p stai long')}.")
qa(None, "What are marginal and conditional R2?",
   f"Marginal R2 = variance explained by the fixed effects only (here phase) / total variance. Conditional R2 = variance explained by fixed effects plus the random intercepts / total. For DRSP psychological (longitudinal, {nlw}) marginal R2 is {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 DRSP long')} and conditional R2 {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'conditional_R2', '{:.3f}', label='conditional R2 DRSP long')}: most of the explained variance is each woman's own baseline. For an unseen woman only the marginal part is available, which is why prediction for new women looks so much worse than the conditional fit suggests.")
qa(None, "Why did you split estradiol into within-woman and between-woman parts, and what did you find?",
   f"A single estradiol coefficient mixes two questions: 'when her own estradiol is higher than usual, are her symptoms different?' (within) and 'do women with higher average estradiol have different symptoms?' (between). In the longitudinal sample ({nlw}) DRSP psychological: within-woman b = {mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2 within/between', 'zw_estradiol')}, between-woman b = {mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2 within/between', 'zb_estradiol')}. So the association is entirely within women. That is why estradiol cannot help predict an unseen woman: the useful signal is relative to her own baseline. Caveat: within a woman, estradiol is confounded with phase; with phase in the model the within coefficient shrinks to {e2g('long', 'DRSP_psychisch', 'E2 + phase')}.")
qa(None, "How do your permutation tests work and why shuffle within women?",
   f"If phase had no effect, the labels of a woman's sessions would be interchangeable. So I shuffle the phase labels among each woman's own sessions (repeat-measured women) or among single-session women, recompute the test statistic, and repeat thousands of times; the p-value is the share of shuffles at least as extreme as the real data (with +1 so p is never zero). Shuffling within women keeps the dependence structure intact. For the within-woman estradiol test the estradiol values are shuffled within woman; for the between test the women's mean estradiol values are shuffled across women. Example (longitudinal, {nlw}): phase on DRSP psychological p = {pp('long', 'drsp_psych', 'phase omnibus F (within-woman)')}; between-woman estradiol on DRSP psychological p = {pp('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2 between-woman slope on woman means (|slope|)', '{:.3f}')}.")
qa(None, "Holm versus FDR: what did you correct and what happened?",
   f"I made {fam('C vs A', 'n')} paired comparisons of phase + hormones versus phase alone. Holm controls the chance of any false positive (family-wise error) and is valid under any dependence, but it is strict. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR controls the expected share of false discoveries among the findings and is more permissive (it assumes a certain kind of positive dependence). Counts of comparisons with p < 0.05: raw {fam('C vs A', 'raw', 'p_two_sided < 0.05')}, Holm {fam('C vs A', 'holm', 'p_holm < 0.05')}, FDR {fam('C vs A', 'bh', 'p_fdr_bh < 0.05')}. All those hits were cases where adding hormones made predictions worse; for the claim that hormones improve prediction the smallest one-sided p was {calc('min raw one-sided p C vs A', df(MC)[df(MC).family=='C vs A'].p_improve_one_sided.min(), '{:.3f}', MC, 'min p_improve_one_sided in family C vs A')} even before correction, so the conclusion does not depend on the correction.")
qa(None, "How do your statistical results compare with the authors'?",
   f"On the like-for-like longitudinal sample ({nlw}) the pattern is reproduced: positive affect is higher in the peri-ovulatory phase than at menses (mine {mxci('long', 'pos_affect', 'phase', 'ph1')}; authors' reported {ref('pa_periov')}), DRSP symptoms are lower than at menses (mine {mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'phase', 'ph1')}; authors' reported {ref('drsp_periov')}), estradiol relates to DRSP (mine {mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2*P', 'z_estradiol')}; authors' reported {ref('drsp_e2')}), and hormone means by phase match theirs. Differences: the phase x PSST interaction is weaker for my psychological score (p {val(MX, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych', model='phase x PSST', term='interaction joint (chi2 df=2)'), 'p', 'p', label='PSST interaction p, long')} versus the authors' reported {ref('psst_interaction_p')}), probably because my DRSP score and model differ from theirs; I use Wald z tests instead of nlme t-tests. The authors' numbers are reference only and are labelled as such in the report.")
qa("D. Credibility checks and limitations",
   "What is the negative control and what is its one caveat?",
   f"Emotion-recognition accuracy should not depend on phase or hormones (the paper found no link). If my pipeline predicted it well, I would suspect a bug or leakage. For accuracy the control passed: R2_oos for all hormone-containing sets was between {calc('negctrl accuracy min R2_oos', hs.R2_oos.min(), '{:+.3f}', N6, 'min R2_oos, target er_accuracy, sets B/C/D')} and {calc('negctrl accuracy max R2_oos', hs.R2_oos.max(), '{:+.3f}', N6, 'max R2_oos, target er_accuracy, sets B/C/D')}. The caveat is speed (median reaction time): the best R2_oos was {calc('negctrl speed max R2_oos', best.R2_oos, '{:+.3f}', N6, 'row with max R2_oos, target er_speed, sets B/C/D')}, estradiol related to speed in the mixed model (nominal p = {val(MM6, dict(sample='long', outcome='er_speed'), 'E2_p', '{:.3f}', label='ER speed E2 p long')}, longitudinal, {nlw}), and {n_holm_neg} of the {n_b_neg} hormone-vs-baseline tests survived Holm correction (an RMSE change of {calc('negctrl Holm survivor dRMSE (ms)', holm_hit.delta_RMSE, '{:+.2f}', D6, 'row of B vs baseline with p_holm < 0.05')} ms on a baseline error of {val(N6, dict(sample='cross2long', target='er_speed', model='baseline', fset='-'), 'RMSE', '{:.1f}', label='negctrl speed baseline RMSE cross2long')} ms, practically nil). My speed measure differs from the authors' (no outlier cut, all emotions pooled), so I report it as an honest caveat rather than an effect.")
qa(None, "What is the phase sanity check, what is chance, and what does the confusion matrix show?",
   f"As a positive control I predicted the cycle phase (menses / peri-ovulatory / luteal) from the two hormones. Chance for balanced accuracy (mean of per-class recall) is 1/3 = {calc('chance', 1/3, '{:.3f}', 'definition', '1/3 for 3 classes')}; the majority-class baseline scores exactly that. Logistic regression reached {ci(B6, dict(sample='pooled', model='logreg'), 'bal_acc', '{:.2f}', lo='bal_acc_lo', hi='bal_acc_hi', label='balacc pooled logreg')} ({npw}) and {ci(B6, dict(sample='long', model='logreg'), 'bal_acc', '{:.2f}', lo='bal_acc_lo', hi='bal_acc_hi', label='balacc long logreg')} (longitudinal, {nlw}); a label-permutation test gave p = {ptt('pooled')}, the smallest possible with {NPERM()} shuffles. In the pooled confusion matrix the luteal phase is mostly right ({cmx('pooled', 'luteal', 'luteal')} of {int(sum(float(cmx('pooled', 'luteal', p_)) for p_ in ['menses', 'peri-ov', 'luteal']))} luteal rows, thanks to progesterone), while {cmx('pooled', 'menses', 'peri-ov')} menses rows were called peri-ovulatory and {cmx('pooled', 'peri-ov', 'menses')} peri-ovulatory rows were called menses (estradiol overlaps). So hormones do carry phase information, which makes the lack of added mood information more meaningful.")
qa(None, "What are the main limitations?",
   f"(1) Small sample: only {n_wom('long')} women have repeated sessions and {n_wom('cross')} have one; intervals are wide and small effects cannot be excluded. (2) Noisy hormones: salivary immunoassays have limited validity (the authors say so too), so true hormone effects may be attenuated. (3) Concurrent, not forecasting: hormones and mood are measured in the same session, so models describe a state rather than predict the future. (4) Self-report overlap between BDI, BAI, PSST, DRSP and STAI. (5) My DRSP target, models and tests differ somewhat from the authors'. (6) No external validation and many looks at the data (corrections applied only to the paired hormone comparisons). (7) Cycle phase labels come from the study schedule.")
qa(None, "Is this a clinical tool, and what would you do next?",
   f"No. It is a learning project on one open dataset; nothing here should be used to diagnose, treat or advise anyone, and any demo must say so. Next steps that would matter scientifically: a prospective forecasting design (predict tomorrow's mood from today's data and the woman's own history), more women with several cycles each, better hormone assays, and external validation on another study. The extensions I would run next (PCA of the correlated hormones, a Bayesian ridge with predictive intervals, a Gaussian Bayes classifier for phase) would add understanding of uncertainty, not new clinical claims.")
assert qn[0] == 30, qn[0]
for _f in sorted((ROOT / "src").glob("ext_*_viva.py")): exec(open(_f).read())
V[0] = f"# Viva questions and answers ({qn[0]})\n"
(ROOT / "docs/viva_qa.md").write_text("\n".join(V))
